When interpreted source handles SOME of an event's branches and an unhandled one fires, does the outcome leak to the caller or is it an error?

Measured 2026-08-07. The interpreter answers this THREE different ways depending
on how many arms you wrote, and the three do not line up:

  A. ZERO arms, `bad` fires   -> result branch=[bad]    the outcome leaks to the
                                                        caller, intact
  B. ONE arm (`| ok`), `bad` fires -> DISPATCH ERROR: NoBranchMatch
  C. ONE arm (`| ok`), `ok` fires  -> the arm runs, and the result comes back
                                      with branch=[] — the name is LOST

Whatever the ruling, A and B cannot both be right. Handling FEWER branches
(none) is currently more permissive than handling SOME (one), which is backwards
from every other rule in the language: in compiled Koru, KORU022 requires every
branch to be handled, and partial handling is the error. Here partial handling
is the only shape that fails.

C is a separate defect and does not need a ruling: an arm that fires should not
erase the branch name from the result. It is why 430_025 prints "Success:
branch " with nothing after it.

THE QUESTION, and it is a design call, not a bug report. Lars raised it as
"it feels like some branches should be able to leak like that" — an interpreted
flow whose unhandled outcome, with its payload, becomes the `run` call's result
for the HOST program to handle. That is coherent and rather elegant: the
interpreted fragment handles what it knows how to handle and hands the rest
back up, so the host's own `| result` arm is the outer handler. It also matches
what A already does.

The alternative is to make the interpreter enforce KORU022's rule at dispatch
time — every declared branch must have an arm, or the run is refused before
anything executes — which is stricter than today and would make A an error too.

Two things worth weighing:
  - The resource bridge makes this sharper than a taste question. A leaked
    branch may carry a HANDLE. If an unhandled `| opened` outcome returns to
    the host, who owns the obligation — and does auto-discharge see it?
    Nothing about the current behaviour answers that, and A leaks today.
  - An LLM writing the source will under-handle constantly. Which of these two
    shapes it meets is the difference between "the agent's fragment composes
    with its host" and "the agent gets NoBranchMatch and must be taught the
    full branch set of every verb".

expected.txt asserts the LEAK reading (B behaves like A) plus the branch name
being preserved in C, so this test is red today and flips if that is the ruling.
If the ruling goes the other way, expected.txt is rewritten to assert refusal
and A changes with it.

THE SHELL CASE, added 2026-08-07 — this is what the ruling is FOR.

Lars: a line you would type at a prompt —

    std/io:file.read(file: "chat.txt") | ok lines |> std/io:print.ln("Got files")

happy path only, on ONE line, and if it fails the interpreter STOPS and hands
back the selected branch and the resource handle instead of making you write
`| failed errno`. Exhaustiveness is a property a PROGRAM needs because nothing
is watching; in an INTERACTIVE session the human at the prompt IS the outer
handler. Every shell already works this way — a non-zero exit is surfaced, not
required to be handled at the call site.

It fails twice today. Measured:

  one line, head + arm  ->  PARSE ERROR: No flow found in source
  split, only `| ok`    ->  DISPATCH ERROR: NoBranchMatch   (case B above)

So the ONLY lenient shape is writing no arms at all, which is the exact inverse
of what a shell wants: you may have the leak, but only if you give up the happy
path. Note the parse failure is separable and has no semantic content — a head
and its arms on one line is a parser question, useful on its own, and cheap.

WHY THIS MAKES THE RULING EASIER RATHER THAN HARDER. It is not "should the
interpreter be lenient" but "who is the outer handler". At a prompt it is the
human. Under kopium's bridge it is the MODEL, which under-handles constantly and
gets the outcome fed back as a message — an unhandled `| failed` returning
"here is what happened, you did not handle it" is a better teaching signal than
a refusal. Inside a compiled program running untrusted config, there is nobody,
and strict is right.

That argues leniency is a MODE, not the interpreter's nature, and `run` already
has the shape for it: it takes fail_fast, shape, budget and auto_discharge
today. One more input selects totality, the shell passes the lenient one, and a
program embedding `run` keeps the strict default.

WHAT THE LEAK MUST CARRY, and this is the piece that does not exist. "Stops with
the resource handle" means both the branch that fired AND the live handles. If
the line is `open(...) |> append(...) | ok |> ...` and `append` fails unhandled,
you are holding what `open` gave you. `run` returns `handles` as a COUNT today —
and frag-a-handle-count-is-not-a-capability-check already says a count is not
the thing. A shell needs them NAMED, the way kopium's bridge names note_0, so
the next line can address what the last one left behind. That is the same
surface the bridge already mints and nothing currently returns.
